# veh-cluster corpus notes

The ridl reference **Appendix A**, compiled. `cluster/appendix-a.ridl` is the
appendix text verbatim — no line is adapted for the corpus — so every
diagnostic it draws is a fact about the reference, recorded below rather than
edited away. `common/` supplies the `veh.common` vocabulary Appendix A imports;
it is the typl reference Appendix B example, the same text the `veh-common`
entry compiles standalone.

`cluster/services.ridl` is authored on top: Appendix A declares no `service`,
and §14.5 needs both service forms exercised.

## What the entry covers

- **Both service forms.** Two named-reference services over one interface shape
  (`veh.cluster.status` and `veh.cluster.standby` both realize `VehicleStatus`)
  and one inline shape (`veh.hvac.cabin`).
- **The inline shape carries the whole surface.** A service's inline shape is
  an `Interface` stored under `Service.shape`, outside `Package.interfaces`, so
  a consumer that walks `package.interfaces` alone skips it silently — the
  shape of five separate E2 defects. `veh.hvac.cabin` therefore holds all five
  interaction kinds, every timing mode, contracts including one that reads
  `result`, an inline `T | E` fallible return, streams in both positions, a
  `reserved` tombstone, a deprecated member, and both `fixed` forms. The IR,
  Rust and TypeScript snapshots record what each consumer made of them.
- **Every timing mode.** Strict periodic (`@1s`, `@500us`), a range with both
  bounds, lower-bound-only, upper-bound-only, and one untimed interaction whose
  bounds come from the package `[defaults].timing`. The IR carries resolved
  microsecond bounds in every case, with `default_applied` set only on the last.
- **Ordinals with a gap, in both tombstone forms.** `reserved setEcoMode` holds
  ordinal 8 in the inline shape and `reserved resetCounters` holds ordinal 6 in
  `VehicleStatus`. `cluster/evolution.ridl` adds the *nameless* form,
  `reserved <ordinal>`, in a named interface and in an inline shape — the two
  stores lower through separate loops. The load-bearing consumer is
  `crates/ridl-diff/src/classify.rs::slots`, which folds tombstones into the ordinal
  sequence so a freed slot never looks free; without that a new interaction
  reusing a retired wire identity classifies as a clean append.
- **The whole guaranteed expression subset.** Comparison, boolean connectives,
  enum access, tuple-field access and duration comparison come from Appendix A
  and the inline shape; conjunction, arithmetic over a parameter, arithmetic
  over `result`, and a reference to a declared `const` (`MAX_FAN`) are the three
  members appended at the end of `veh.hvac.cabin`. They are appended rather than
  inserted so the ordinals above — and the synthesized transport identity
  `veh.hvac.cabin#13` — do not move.

  _What this entry does not isolate._ Appendix A's
  `require position != GearPosition.PARK` references an **imported** name from a
  contract clause, which is the shape of the TYPL-007 defect #174 fixed: the
  resolver's use-collection walked type references and literals but not
  expressions, so a name reached only from a contract was reported unused. This
  entry did not surface it, because `GearPosition` is also the parameter's type
  two lines above and the type reference alone marks it used. Isolating it needs
  an import that a contract clause is the *only* reader of, which is a fixture
  rather than a corpus entry:
  `an_import_used_only_in_a_contract_clause_is_not_unused` in `resolve.rs`. The
  `const` reference here, `MAX_FAN`, is declared in `services.ridl` rather than
  imported, so it never reached the rule either.
- **Cross-package references.** Appendix A's five imports resolve through the
  workspace member, and `ridl.std` types (`Label`, `Message`, `Timestamp`,
  `Version`) resolve without an import.
- **`internal` on the interaction layer.** `cluster/internal-shape.ridl`
  declares `internal interface WheelDiagnostics` beside a public
  `interface WheelSummary`, so the generated output shows both side by side.
  This is the file that caught the visibility defect described under
  "`internal` was dropped by both backends" below; the snapshots now pin the
  repaired output. `WheelDiagnostics` also names `internal type RawTickCount`
  as a payload, a parameter and a return, which makes it the **legal** case of
  the exposure rule issue #161 closed: an `internal` interface over an
  `internal` payload must keep compiling, and both sides must generate
  `pub(crate)`. `rustc_accepts` denies `private_interfaces` by name, so that is
  a compile proof rather than a snapshot comparison — with the payload public,
  as it was before, the same proof passes even with the #160 defect back in
  place.
- **The generated code compiles, in both backends.**
  `veh_cluster_generated_rust_compiles_with_rustc` composes the two packages as
  sibling modules and runs `rustc` over the result;
  `veh_cluster_generated_typescript_type_checks` runs the emitted modules
  through `tsc --noEmit --strict` alongside the generated `ridl.std` module.
  Both snapshots are therefore proofs rather than transcripts, which is what the
  E2 exit criterion — IR neutrality demonstrated by two backends — actually
  asks for. The tsc proof skips with a printed notice when no tsc is
  discoverable (the convention `crates/ridl-backend-ts` set), so Node stays
  optional for `cargo test`; the generated-source markers are asserted either
  way, so it never passes by type-checking nothing.

  _Known limitation._ Under a plain `cargo test` libtest captures the skip
  notice, so a skipped run reports a bare `ok` and is indistinguishable from a
  checked one without `--nocapture`. This matches the `crates/ridl-backend-ts`
  precedent and the markers keep the test non-vacuous either way. If it is
  closed later, the shape is an opt-in `RIDL_REQUIRE_TSC=1` that turns the skip
  into a failure.

## Diagnostics the entry draws, and why they are kept

The entry has no errors, so it is a full-pipeline golden. It carries two
warnings, both of them true statements about the source:

- **TYPL-007, unused import `MAX_SPEED`** — Appendix A imports
  `veh.common.MAX_SPEED` and never uses it. A fact about the appendix text.
- **RIDL-100, untimed event** — deliberate, in `services.ridl`: it is the only
  way to exercise the default-applied path end to end.

**RIDL-308 no longer fires here, and that is the recorded change.** Appendix A
wrote `query getFaultPage(filter: DiagFilter): FaultPageResult` over a named
`union FaultPageResult`, which drew the lint. General form §6.1 made the inline
`FaultPage | DiagError` spelling canonical in return position after the appendix
was written, and the E2 close-out adopted it in the reference text (ADR-0008
decisions 1 and 19); the appendix now writes the inline form and the union
declaration is deleted with it, because adopting the inline return leaves the
union unreferenced and no pass reports an unused declaration — TYPL-007 covers
unused imports only. RIDL-308 keeps a living example in `ridl-diag-showcase`,
which provokes it independently.

## Findings this entry pins

Nothing here is worked around. Each is written the way the toolchain behaves, so
the snapshots pin the behaviour and a repair shows up as a snapshot change.
Several have since been repaired that way. A repaired one is marked
**Status: fixed** and its history is kept below rather than deleted, because
what the corpus found and why the coverage exists is part of what the corpus is
for — a section that silently disappears teaches nothing.

### An array of tuples was emitted as a TypeScript block — fixed in #177

**Status: fixed.** Recorded because the coverage that now guards it was added
here, and because the reason it went undetected is a statement about the corpus
rather than about the backend.

`RawWheelSpan.bursts` is an array whose **element** is a tuple. Until it was
added, no corpus entry held that combination: `veh-common` has an array of a
named type (`readings : [Speed; 8]`) and a bare tuple field
(`range : (min, max)`), and the interaction layer has an array of tuples in a
`fixed` payload, which generates a type but no init. So no generated init ever
placed an object literal in a concise arrow body, and the TypeScript backend
emitted the `Array.from` callback as
`() => { first: initRawTickCount(), latest: initRawTickCount() }`, with no
parentheses around the object literal.

A concise arrow body that starts with `{` is a **block**, not an object
literal. The one-field spelling in the issue parses as a labelled statement and
the callback returns `undefined` (TS2322); the two-field spelling here is a
syntax error (TS1005). The repair parenthesises such a body —
`() => ({ … })` — in `crates/ridl-backend-ts/src/lib.rs::array_from`, which is
now the only place that backend emits an arrow function.

`span` beside it is the control: a tuple in **direct field position** is
emitted as a plain object literal, was correct throughout, and stays
unparenthesised. Both forms are in one generated init function, so the
TypeScript snapshot shows them next to each other. The Rust backend was
unaffected — it emits `::core::array::from_fn(|_| …::default())` over the
induced element struct — and the snapshot records that too.

`veh_cluster_generated_typescript_type_checks` is the proof: with the field
present and the repair reverted, `tsc --strict` refuses the emitted module.

### `internal` was dropped by both backends — found here, fixed in #160

**Status: fixed.** Recorded because this entry is what found it.

`internal` is accepted on an `interface` (on an interaction or a service it is a
parse error), the checker honours it, and the IR records it faithfully:
`WheelDiagnostics` lowers with `visibility: 2` where `WheelSummary` and
`VehicleStatus` carry `1`. `ridl diff` models it too. Between PRs #155/#156
and #160, both backends then threw it away:

- **Rust** emitted `pub trait WheelDiagnosticsConsumer`,
  `pub trait WheelDiagnosticsProvider`, `pub const WHEEL_DIAGNOSTICS_TIMING` and
  `pub const WHEEL_DIAGNOSTICS_CONTRACTS` — byte-for-byte the same visibility as
  the public `WheelSummary`.
- **TypeScript** emitted `export interface WheelDiagnosticsConsumer`,
  `export interface WheelDiagnosticsProvider`, `export const
  wheelDiagnosticsTiming` and `export const wheelDiagnosticsContracts`.

A package-private contract shape leaked into both generated public surfaces — a
violation of **ADR-0002 §8** and **ADR-0008 decision 7**, not a gap in the
design. The control was in the same snapshots and is what bounded the
diagnosis: `internal struct RawWheelFrame` (typl, in `common/`) was already
correct in both backends, so the fault was the interaction layer specifically
and not `internal` in general.

**How it was found, and why that matters here.** The defect shipped in two
merged PRs and survived review of both backends. Nothing caught it until an
`internal interface` was added to this corpus — which is the argument for the
corpus in one sentence: a construct that no unit test exercised, compiled
through the real driver, beside its public counterpart, in output a human reads.

**The repair (#160).** Both backends now derive the visibility once per
interface from `Interface.visibility` and apply it to all four generated names,
so the two backends agree name for name. Deliberately unaffected, and asserted
as such: the interaction vocabulary (emitted once per module, shared by every
interface in it); the service table, since a service takes no `internal`
modifier (§14.5) and an inline shape's `VISIBILITY_UNSPECIFIED` maps to public;
and the Rust tuple structs an interaction position induces, which follow the
typl rule for a tuple under an `internal` declaration — TypeScript has no
counterpart, since a tuple is an inline object type there.

`internal_on_an_interface_is_package_private_in_both_backends` is now the
regression guard. It asserts all four names per backend in their fixed
spellings **and** the absence of all four leaked spellings, with the
`internal struct` control beside them. The four-per-backend shape is
deliberate: the first report of the defect named only the two faces and the
timing constant, and the contracts constant was found by enumerating what each
backend generates rather than by reading the report.

Two residues #160 could not reach, since they were checker behaviour rather than
codegen — issue **#161, now closed**. TYPL-005 ran over typl declarations only,
so a public `service` could publish an `internal` interface and a public
`interface` could carry an `internal` payload type, both undiagnosed. The second
was the sharper one: it made the Rust backend emit a `pub` trait over a
`pub(crate)` type, which draws rustc's `private_interfaces` lint and becomes a
hard build failure for any consumer building with `-D warnings`.

The repair moved the rule out of the typl definition lowering and into a
whole-file pass over the top-level items, so no declaration kind can escape it
by being added later; the service half took its own code, RIDL-143, because a
service cannot be made `internal` and so TYPL-005's second remedy does not
exist for it. The refusals are provoked in `ridl-diag-showcase`; the legal case
is here, in `cluster/internal-shape.ridl`.

### An inline shape carries no `Interface.visibility`

`Service.shape.Inline` lowers with `visibility: 0` (`VISIBILITY_UNSPECIFIED`)
where every named interface carries `1` (public). The reading recorded here, now
that the IR snapshot is golden: an inline shape has no name and is not
addressable as a type, so it has no visibility of its own — the enclosing
`Service` carries the authoritative one (`1` here), and a service cannot be
`internal` at all. A consumer must therefore read `Service.visibility`, never
`Service.shape.Inline.visibility`. If a consumer ever needs the field, the
inline shape should mirror its service rather than stay unspecified.
`ridl_ir::v2::InterfaceShape::visibility` is where that rule now lives, so a
consumer reading it through `Package::shapes` cannot get the unspecified value
by accident.

### The sixth inline-shape instance: RIDL-407 rendered without a span — fixed

Found by this corpus and pinned in `crates/ridl/tests/baseline_desk.rs`:
`DeclIndex::build` in `crates/ridl/src/main.rs` recorded inline-shape
*members*, but built the shape-level fallback — used when the named interaction
no longer exists in source — from `source.interfaces()` only. An interaction
removed from an inline shape therefore produced a RIDL-407 warning with no
file, no line and no snippet, where the same removal from a named interface
fell back to the interface name.

It was the sixth and last instance of one root cause: a consumer walking
`package.interfaces` (or `source.interfaces()`) as if it were the complete set
of interface bodies, when a service's inline shape lives outside it. All six
are closed. The structural repair is a single walk on each side —
`ridl_ir::v2::Package::shapes` and `ridl_syntax::ast::SourceFile::shapes` —
which yields a named interface and a service's inline shape alike, carrying the
identity name (the interface's own, or the service's dotted one) so no consumer
reads the inline shape's empty `Interface.name`, and the owning `Service` so no
consumer reads its unspecified `Interface.visibility` either (see the section
above). `DeclIndex` now walks `SourceFile::shapes()` and the inline-shape
warning spans the service's dotted name;
`inline_shape_removal_spans_the_service_name` pins that.

### A non-integer `reserved` literal destroyed the retired identity — fixed

**Status: fixed in #174.** Recorded because this entry is what found it.

typl Appendix E defines `reserved = "reserved" ( camelCase_id | int_lit )`, but
`Parser::reserved_entry` fell through to `self.literal()`, which admits **any**
literal shape. `reserved "oldName"`, `reserved true` and `reserved 1.5`
therefore compiled with **no diagnostic at all**.

**The consequence, which is why it was worth fixing.** Silent acceptance was
not the cost — `lower_reserved` keeps a `Name` or an integer and nothing else,
so an ungrammatical entry lowered to `Reserved { name: None, value: None }`.
The slot still consumed its ordinal, but **the retired identity the tombstone
exists to record was destroyed in the IR**. In an `enum` body the retired wire
value was lost outright. A dangling-tombstone report (TYPL-211) then has
nothing left to name, and a publisher who typed a quote around a retired name
got a provenance-free tombstone with no warning at all.

The identity-reuse guarantee itself survived throughout:
`crates/ridl-diff/src/classify.rs::slots` maps a nameless tombstone to
`("", ordinal)`, which never equals a real interaction name. So this was a
provenance hole, not a wire-safety hole — which is exactly why nothing else
caught it. Input outside the grammar accepted in silence is the failure family
this corpus exists to find.

**The extent, once looked for.** The defect was reported for the interface
body. `reserved_entry` is one production reached by five bodies — `struct`,
`enum` and `union` through `block_body`, `interface` and a `service` inline
shape through `interface_body` — and all five were affected, under both
profiles. Walking the token inventory rather than the reported literals also
turned up `reserved -MAX_GEAR`: `literal_len_at` counts `-` plus any value
token and `Ident` is a value token, so a negated constant reference parsed as a
`Literal` and lowered to nothing in the same way.

**The repair (#174).** The literal shapes outside the production now draw
**FORM-102** at the parser, and the refused token is held in an `ErrorNode`
inside the `ReservedEntry`, so the tree stays lossless, the entry keeps its
place as a member, and the member after it keeps its ordinal. FORM-102 was
reused rather than a new code allocated: a token is present and the grammar has
no production admitting it there, which is what FORM-102 means, and allocating
a code is an ADR decision (ADR-0008 decision 13). The two grammatical forms are
unchanged, negative integers included. `reserved` with nothing after it stays
FORM-101, with its message reworded to name both admitted forms.

The pinning test `reserved_accepts_ungrammatical_literals_and_discards_them` is
replaced by `reserved_rejects_ungrammatical_literals` — which asserts the code,
the message, and that the ordinals around the refused entry do not move — and
by `reserved_accepts_both_grammatical_forms_in_every_body`, which guards
against narrowing the production past what Appendix E writes. Both drive
`compile` directly, so the corpus files themselves stay grammatical. The
recovery shape is pinned in the err corpus by
`test_data/parser/err/reserved_ungrammatical_literal.{typl,ridl}` under
`crates/ridl-syntax/`.

**Residual, still open: `reserved <int>` in a `struct` or `union` body.**
Appendix E's production is body-agnostic, so refusing this at the parser would
be wrong — the residual is a missing *checker* rule, and #174 did not add one.
The entry compiles clean and lowers to
`Reserved { name: None, value: Some(n) }`, which is the same empty provenance
the ungrammatical literals produced: a struct field and a union arm carry no
explicit value, so nothing can ever match `value`, and `name: None` leaves
TYPL-210 with no retired name to match.

The scope is exactly those two bodies, which was established by running each
form rather than by reading the grammar:

- `struct` / `union`, **name** form — TYPL-210 fires on a field or arm that
  re-declares the retired name. Functional.
- `enum`, **name** form — TYPL-210 fires on a value re-declaring the name.
  Functional. **integer** form — TYPL-210 fires on a value re-using the wire
  number. Functional; this is the form typl §7.4 documents for enums.
- `interface` / inline shape, **integer** form — not a hole. The nameless
  spelling is the deliberate second form: `cluster/evolution.ridl` carries it
  and documents the `Reserved { name: None, value: Some(n) }` lowering as "a
  slot that records the ordinal it protects and nothing else", and `parity.rs`'s
  `MUST_COVER` guards it as "the other grammatical form (typl §7.4)".
- `struct` / `union`, **integer** form — inert. The residual.

It is recorded rather than repaired for the same reason FORM-109 and TYPL-010
were not taken in #174: refusing it needs a new TYPL-2xx code, and allocating a
code is an ADR decision (ADR-0008 decision 13). The repo already anticipated the
rule — `crates/ridl-syntax/test_data/parser/ok/grammar_overapprox.typl` carries
`struct LooseReserved { reserved 7 }` under a header reading "the parser accepts
these and the checker narrows them later (typl reference §16 — TYPL-2xx and
friends)". The narrowing was never written.
`reserved_integer_in_a_struct_or_union_is_inert` pins the residual and fails
with instructions when the rule lands.

### Imports bind at package scope; a repeated import was an error — fixed

**Status: fixed in #174.** Recorded because this entry is what found it.

The scope rule is unchanged and still holds: imports bind per package, not per
file (ADR-0002 §1, "files within a package are purely organisational"). What
was a defect is what the toolchain did with a repeat.

`services.ridl` once imported only `Ratio`, though it references `Speed` and
`Temperature` too, because `appendix-a.ridl` already imports those — and, at
the time this entry was written, importing them a second time was not merely
redundant but a hard **error**: TYPL-006, "conflicting import … alias one of
the colliding imports". `apply_imports` counted local-name collisions without
comparing what each import named, so two identical lines collided with each
other, across files of one package and within a single file alike.

**The fix-it made it worse.** "Alias one of the colliding imports" is written
for two *different* symbols competing for one local name, which is not the
situation. Applying it to an identical repeat either trades the error for
TYPL-007 (unused import) or forks one symbol into two local names. Deleting the
repeat — the remedy that works — was never offered.

**The repair (#174).** An import collides only when it claims a local name that
the first import of that name bound to a **different target**, meaning a
different package path or a different symbol. A genuine collision keeps firing
TYPL-006 with its message and its aliasing remedy unchanged, and the first
import still wins the binding.

An identical repeat is **silent**, in one file and across the files of a
package alike. A distinct duplicate-import warning was considered and not
taken: under ADR-0002 §1 a package's imports are a set, so repeating one adds
nothing to it, and a rule that fired only when two lines share a file would
make the file boundary semantically significant, which §1 denies — while a rule
that fired across files would report the ordinary habit of writing each file's
imports at its own head. TYPL-007 could not be reused, since the repeated
import's symbol *is* used. TYPL-010 is free in the module band and would be the
right home if the project ever wants the notice; allocating a code is an ADR
decision (ADR-0008 decision 13).

**The entry now closes it in source too.** `services.ridl` imports `Speed` and
`Temperature`, which it references and which it had to omit while a repeat was
an error, so the repaired behaviour is pinned end to end: two identical
repeated imports in a real multi-file package, compiled through the real
driver, drawing nothing. The whole cost was one line number in
`corpus__diagnostics@veh-cluster.snap`, where the existing RIDL-100 warning
moved from line 83 to 87; no diagnostic was added or removed. It is not a
prose-only record — reverting the resolver fix turns five corpus and parity
tests red from the source change alone.

`resolve.rs` carries the unit guards for the shapes a golden entry cannot hold
at once: `an_identical_repeated_import_is_not_a_collision`,
`an_identical_import_repeated_across_files_of_a_package_is_not_a_collision`,
`a_repeated_import_under_one_alias_is_not_a_collision`, and
`a_repeat_between_two_genuine_collisions_still_flags_only_the_conflict`, which
asserts the span so a repeat cannot be the line flagged.

**A second defect surfaced when this one was repaired.** TYPL-008 (needless
import alias) counted import *lines* per base name, so repeating an aliased
import made its own base name look shared and suppressed the warning: two
identical `import x.Speed as VehSpeed` lines produced no diagnostic at all,
where one line correctly draws TYPL-008. It was invisible while TYPL-006 fired
on the repeat and went live the moment TYPL-006 went silent. `base_name_sources`
now counts distinct package paths, so an alias is needed only when the base name
would name something else without it. Both directions are guarded:
`a_repeated_aliased_import_still_draws_the_needless_alias_warning` and
`an_alias_over_two_different_targets_is_still_needed`.
Recorded because it is the reason a repeated aliased import is not silent: the
alias warning is the remaining signal on a line that binds nothing new.
